The scanner runs one loop over a transition table, 40 states by 29
character classes. The table gets dumped straight from the running code
with `novac --dump-dfa`, so the csv next to this file is exactly what the
code does, not something retyped by hand.

Every character gets sorted into a class first: digit, letter, quote,
backslash, one class per operator character, EOF, and OTHER for anything
that's not actually part of Nova. OTHER is why something like `@` just
errors out.

The states break down into a few groups: whitespace and comments (get
skipped, produce no token), numbers, identifiers, strings, one pair of
states for each two-character operator like `<`/`<=` or `=`/`==`/`=>`,
one state per punctuation mark that can't turn into anything longer, and
end of input.

Scanning works the same way every time: look at the next character, look
up the current state and that character's class in the table, move to
wherever it points. If that lands on a state that counts as done, keep
it in mind as the best match so far but don't stop, keep reading in case
there's more. Once there's nowhere left to go, stop and report whatever
was last remembered as done.

That's also why something like `=` can turn into `==` or `=>` without
writing any lookahead by hand — `AfterEquals` already accepts a plain
`=`, but it also checks whether another `=` or a `>` shows up right
after, and only falls back to the plain `=` if neither one does. The
same idea covers `<`/`<=`, `>`/`>=`, `&`/`&&`, `|`/`||`, `.`/`..`, and
`-`/`->`.

There are two ways scanning can fail: a character with nowhere to go at
all (a bad symbol, or a lone `!` since Nova only has `!=` and not a bare
`!`), or reaching the end of the file while still inside a string that
never got closed.

Strings needed one extra state for escapes. A `"` normally ends a
string, but hitting a `\` first sends the scanner into a state that just
takes whatever character comes next no matter what it is, even another
`"`, then goes back to reading the string normally. That's the whole
reason `\"` doesn't close a string early. What the escape actually turns
into, an actual newline for `\n` and so on, gets sorted out afterward,
once the string has already been matched in full.

Keywords work off the same identifier state rather than getting states
of their own, since there's nothing different about the shape of `fn`
compared to `foo`. The scanner just matches the identifier first and
checks it against a short keyword list afterward, swapping the token
type if it matches.

Every state, class, and transition is laid out in full in dfa_table.csv.
